❓Question
You are given a 1-indexed sorted array numbers and a target. Find two numbers whose sum equals the target and return their 1-based indices.
You cannot use the same element twice, and the solution must use constant extra space.

Example
Input:
numbers = [2,7,11,15], target = 9
Output:
[1,2]
Explanation:
2 + 7 = 9, and their 1-based indices are 1 and 2.

✅Approach
Use the Two Pointer approach:
pointer1 starts from the beginning.
pointer2 starts from the end.
Calculate currentSum.
If currentSum == target, return the two indices.
If currentSum < target, move pointer1 forward to increase the sum.
If currentSum > target, move pointer2 backward to decrease the sum.

Code
class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] numbers, int target) {
        int pointer1 = 0;
        int pointer2 = numbers.length - 1;
        while(pointer1 < pointer2) {
            int currentSum = numbers[pointer1] + numbers[pointer2];
            if(currentSum == target) {
                return new int[] {pointer1 + 1, pointer2 + 1};
            } else if(currentSum < target) {
                pointer1++;
            } else {
                pointer2--;
            }
        }
        return new int[] {-1, -1};
    }
}
Time Complexity
O(n)
Space Complexity
O(1)
